' ============================================================================
' JSON Example in DBasic
' ============================================================================
' What this file teaches:
'   - How to create JSON objects in DBasic
'   - How to read values from JSON using dot notation (like object.property)
'   - How to change (modify) JSON property values after creation
'
' What is JSON?
'   JSON stands for "JavaScript Object Notation." Think of it like a
'   labeled container -- you give each piece of data a name (called a "key")
'   and a value. For example: {name: "John", age: 30} stores a name and
'   an age together in one variable. This is incredibly useful when you
'   need to group related information together.
'
' In DBasic, JSON is a built-in type, so you can create and use JSON
' objects directly without importing any extra packages.
' ============================================================================

SUB Main()
    PRINT "=== JSON in DBasic ==="
    PRINT ""

    ' Create a simple JSON object with three properties:
    '   - "name" is a STRING (text wrapped in quotes)
    '   - "age" is a NUMBER (just a plain number, no quotes)
    '   - "active" is a BOOLEAN (TRUE or FALSE -- like an on/off switch)
    ' The curly braces { } wrap all the key-value pairs together.
    DIM person AS JSON = {name: "John Doe", age: 30, active: TRUE}

    ' Access JSON properties using dot notation.
    ' "person.name" means: "go into the person object and get the name value."
    ' This is just like opening a folder and looking at a specific file inside.
    PRINT "Name: "; person.name
    PRINT "Age: "; person.age
    PRINT "Active: "; person.active

    ' Modify JSON properties -- you can change any value after creation.
    ' Just use the same dot notation on the left side of the equals sign.
    ' Here we are updating the age from 30 to 31 (happy birthday, John!)
    ' and setting active from TRUE to FALSE.
    person.age = 31
    person.active = FALSE
    PRINT ""
    PRINT "After modification:"
    PRINT "Age: "; person.age
    PRINT "Active: "; person.active

    ' Another JSON object -- this time representing application configuration.
    ' JSON is perfect for storing settings like this because each setting
    ' has a clear name and value.
    PRINT ""
    PRINT "=== Configuration Example ==="
    DIM config AS JSON = {host: "localhost", port: 8080, debug: TRUE}

    ' Read each config value using dot notation, just like before.
    ' "config.host" gives us the server address ("localhost")
    ' "config.port" gives us the port number (8080)
    ' "config.debug" gives us whether debug mode is on (TRUE)
    PRINT "Host: "; config.host
    PRINT "Port: "; config.port
    PRINT "Debug: "; config.debug

    PRINT ""
    PRINT "JSON demo complete!"
END SUB
